1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
An outage ticket lands with the wrong team.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
The policy should send it straight to Platform Operations.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
The router gives each team a score. The highest score wins.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
For this ticket, General Support scores 2. Platform scores only 0.25.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
A weight sets how much a feature contributes to a score.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The outage feature is on here, but off for ordinary support.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
A target-only edit improves the outage route.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
But it also changes an ordinary ticket’s scores. That fails the protection rule.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Think of an edit as a direction in weight space.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Choose a direction with zero effect on the protected inputs.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Here, the framework changes only the outage-to-Platform weight: 0.25 becomes about 3.22.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Now Platform wins this ticket. The model changes through an explicit, checked weight edit.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Final recognized outages reach Platform. The sampled protected scores stay unchanged.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
A direct route could avoid a transfer. Handling time and savings have not been measured.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
If a target must also stay fixed, the framework abstains.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
If a new symptom is not recognized, the final check rejects the candidate.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,500
The bound inverse restores the exact parent model. It cannot undo ticket assignments.

18
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The method is generic. This demonstration uses fixed features and a supplied linear model.
